  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 107 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nWednesday through Monday:\n\nStorms linger into Wednesday as low pressure ejects from the\ncentral Plains into the Middle Mississippi Valley. With southern\nWisconsin remaining on the north side of the low and overrunning\nprecipitation from the night before, expecting benign rainfall\nthrough the morning hours. If clearing develops early enough in\nthe day, instability may work back into southern Wisconsin for\nWednesday afternoon and evening, setting the stage for potential\nsevere weather. MUCAPE up to 2000 J/kg is shown on some ensemble\nmembers of GFS and Euro, but will be highly dependent on clearing\nafter early morning showers and storms. Shear remains favorable\nthroughout the day (45-50 kt bulk shear values), with afternoon\nstorms likely forming on the northern edge of upper level ridging\nand moving eastward, leading to perpendicular shear vectors and a\nridge-rider setup. Storms finally exit to the east into Thursday \nmorning, with clearing skies and highs around 80 degrees.\n\nThursday night, high pressure builds back into the western Great\nLakes, bringing light winds, lows in the upper 50s to low 60s.\nHigh pressure lingers through Friday and Friday night with highs\nin the low to mid 80s. Saturday and Sunday, low pressure \ndeveloping in the northern Plains will bring strong southwesterly \nwinds and temperatures in the mid-80s all the way to Lake\nMichigan. A return to showers and storms Sunday through Monday as\na frontal boundary from the low moves southward through Wisconsin,\nalthough confidence in timing and impacts remain uncertain at this\ntime (15-25% PoPs).\n\nMH",
